WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.262
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.488 --> 00:00:07.780
WILLIAM DENNISS: I'm William
Denniss, a product manager

00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:09.340
for GKE Autopilot.

00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:11.500
I'm excited to introduce
a new mode of operation

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:14.382
for Google Kubernetes Engine
that we think you will like.

00:00:14.382 --> 00:00:16.840
If you're new to Kubernetes,
it's the industry-standard way

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:19.120
to deploy and manage
application containers,

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:21.010
making it efficient
to develop and publish

00:00:21.010 --> 00:00:23.140
internet-based applications.

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:25.090
GKE was the first managed
Kubernetes service

00:00:25.090 --> 00:00:27.520
to the market over five years
ago and remains the most

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:30.220
scalable and reliable platform,
with an industry-leading

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:32.537
15,000-node capability.

00:00:32.537 --> 00:00:34.120
And it's brought to
you by a team that

00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:36.520
makes more technical
contributions to the Kubernetes

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:39.100
project than any other company.

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:41.440
Kubernetes gives you a
container application platform

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:43.030
on which to run your services.

00:00:43.030 --> 00:00:45.238
But what does it actually
take to run this platform?

00:00:45.238 --> 00:00:47.530
There's quite a lot of aspects,
from the control plane,

00:00:47.530 --> 00:00:49.720
provisioning, and
management, to worker node

00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:51.280
creation and patching.

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:54.340
Since its launch, GKE has taken
care of most of these elements

00:00:54.340 --> 00:00:55.220
for you.

00:00:55.220 --> 00:00:56.680
But there are a couple
of things that were still

00:00:56.680 --> 00:00:58.900
your responsibility, like
configuring and managing

00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:00.082
node pools.

00:01:00.082 --> 00:01:01.540
We figured out what
you really care

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:04.030
about most is running your
own applications and services

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:07.220
on GKE, and not the configuring
and monitoring of the computer

00:01:07.220 --> 00:01:08.410
they run on.

00:01:08.410 --> 00:01:11.140
So we looked at what
more we could do to help.

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:12.550
That's why I'm
happy to introduce

00:01:12.550 --> 00:01:16.300
GKE Autopilot, a new mode
of operation for GKE.

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:19.030
With Autopilot, the node
configuration and management

00:01:19.030 --> 00:01:21.260
is now our
responsibility, as well,

00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:24.010
so you can focus more on your
own applications and services

00:01:24.010 --> 00:01:25.420
that run on top.

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:28.660
With Autopilot, everything
is autoprovisioned.

00:01:28.660 --> 00:01:31.510
All you need to do is name
the cluster, pick a region,

00:01:31.510 --> 00:01:34.660
set the network, and deploy
your workload with Kubernetes.

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:36.460
And once things are
provisioned, Google

00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:39.520
is responsible for managing the
nodes and keeping them running.

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:42.720
When it comes to the bill,
you pay for pods and not VMs.

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:44.800
Let me drill into each of
these a little further.

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:46.630
I mentioned that with
Autopilot, we have

00:01:46.630 --> 00:01:48.280
autoprovisioned everything.

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:50.800
What this means in practice is
that your pod and deployment

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:53.890
configuration is also the
complete cluster specification.

00:01:53.890 --> 00:01:56.620
We provision compute resources
based on the resource

00:01:56.620 --> 00:01:58.690
needs you specify
in your pod spec.

00:01:58.690 --> 00:02:00.610
There's minimal cluster
setup, with no need

00:02:00.610 --> 00:02:02.560
to figure out what
size and shape of nodes

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:04.370
should be provided
for your workloads.

00:02:04.370 --> 00:02:06.820
We also apply all the latest
security best practices

00:02:06.820 --> 00:02:08.030
automatically.

00:02:08.030 --> 00:02:10.030
You've already been able
to offload many cluster

00:02:10.030 --> 00:02:11.980
operational
responsibilities to Google,

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:14.330
and now this applies
to nodes, as well.

00:02:14.330 --> 00:02:16.240
And since we're now
managing the nodes,

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:18.100
they're more locked
down, which also

00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:21.370
serves to prevent accidental or
intentional modifications that

00:02:21.370 --> 00:02:22.660
can cause issues.

00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:24.130
Even with us
managing everything,

00:02:24.130 --> 00:02:26.588
you can still use features like
maintenance windows and pod

00:02:26.588 --> 00:02:30.170
disruption budgets to govern
how and when updates occur.

00:02:30.170 --> 00:02:32.440
In fact, we're so
confident of our management

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:35.620
of this new platform, that
we're offering a pod-level SLA

00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:37.630
in GKE for the first time.

00:02:37.630 --> 00:02:40.780
This SLA offers three
nines of uptime for pods

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:42.370
running in multiple zones.

00:02:42.370 --> 00:02:44.650
The other major difference
is how you're billed.

00:02:44.650 --> 00:02:47.560
Since you now specify your
resources at a pod level,

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:49.510
that's how you're
billed, as well.

00:02:49.510 --> 00:02:53.440
Your pods are billed per second
for vCPU, memory, and disk

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:54.790
resource requests.

00:02:54.790 --> 00:02:57.730
System components, the
operating system overhead,

00:02:57.730 --> 00:03:00.040
and, most importantly,
unused capacity,

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:01.540
is no longer your concern.

00:03:01.540 --> 00:03:04.060
Once you scale a workload
down, the associated charges

00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:05.560
cease immediately.

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:07.870
GKE's free tier
includes Autopilot,

00:03:07.870 --> 00:03:10.422
giving you one free zone
or Autopilot cluster.

00:03:10.422 --> 00:03:12.505
So you can give it a try
and just pay for the pods

00:03:12.505 --> 00:03:13.570
you deploy.

00:03:13.570 --> 00:03:16.180
Despite being fully
managed, GKE Autopilot

00:03:16.180 --> 00:03:18.610
is still GKE and supports
nearly the full gamut

00:03:18.610 --> 00:03:20.830
of workload capabilities.

00:03:20.830 --> 00:03:23.650
Workloads requiring
StatefulSet and even DaemonSet

00:03:23.650 --> 00:03:26.592
are supported at launch, and
more support is on the roadmap.

00:03:26.592 --> 00:03:28.300
We think you shouldn't
have to compromise

00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:30.880
when it comes to your
Kubernetes application platform

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:33.070
and built Autopilot
with that in mind.

00:03:33.070 --> 00:03:34.690
From the outset, we
said to ourselves

00:03:34.690 --> 00:03:38.620
that Autopilot is GKE, and we
want you to hold us to that.

00:03:38.620 --> 00:03:40.858
Popular administrative
workloads from our partners,

00:03:40.858 --> 00:03:42.400
in areas like logging
and monitoring,

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:45.190
are either supported
now or on the roadmap.

00:03:45.190 --> 00:03:46.918
Basically, the only
workloads we don't

00:03:46.918 --> 00:03:49.210
expect to be compatible with
this new mode of operation

00:03:49.210 --> 00:03:51.680
are custom administrative
workloads that

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:54.430
might be used for low-level
node management tasks,

00:03:54.430 --> 00:03:56.200
exactly the kind of
thing which we hope

00:03:56.200 --> 00:03:58.320
won't be needed in Autopilot.

00:03:58.320 --> 00:03:59.475
And one more thing--

00:03:59.475 --> 00:04:01.350
we've actually been
running a private preview

00:04:01.350 --> 00:04:03.225
for a few months, and
I'm pleased to announce

00:04:03.225 --> 00:04:06.300
that Autopilot is launching as
generally available and ready

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:08.370
for your production workloads.

00:04:08.370 --> 00:04:10.920
With that, I'd like to take you
through a quick demo of what

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:12.063
Autopilot can do.

00:04:12.063 --> 00:04:15.240
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:17.550
To create an Autopilot
cluster, all you need to do

00:04:17.550 --> 00:04:21.783
is give it a name, pick
your favorite region,

00:04:21.783 --> 00:04:24.200
and set the network, if you
have any particular networking

00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:25.437
requirements.

00:04:25.437 --> 00:04:27.770
In my case, I'll leave the
network as the default Public

00:04:27.770 --> 00:04:30.170
cluster and click Create.

00:04:30.170 --> 00:04:31.710
Creation takes a
couple of minutes,

00:04:31.710 --> 00:04:34.340
so let me switch to one
I've created earlier.

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:36.890
To connect to this cluster,
I click the Connect button

00:04:36.890 --> 00:04:39.590
and copy the connection
command to my clipboard.

00:04:39.590 --> 00:04:42.050
Now, from the command line,
I'll paste that command

00:04:42.050 --> 00:04:45.933
to authenticate kubectl so
it can access the cluster.

00:04:45.933 --> 00:04:47.350
The demo I'm going
to deploy today

00:04:47.350 --> 00:04:49.730
is from the GKE
sample repository.

00:04:49.730 --> 00:04:51.640
So let's clone that
repository from GitHub

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:55.760
and change into the
sample directory.

00:04:55.760 --> 00:04:58.300
Finally, I'm going to apply
the Kubernetes configuration

00:04:58.300 --> 00:04:59.620
of the guestbook sample.

00:05:02.510 --> 00:05:04.870
Now, this sample consists
of a PHP frontend

00:05:04.870 --> 00:05:07.430
with a Redis backend.

00:05:07.430 --> 00:05:09.130
If you've deployed
Redis before, you'll

00:05:09.130 --> 00:05:11.813
be aware it does have a
few resource requirements.

00:05:11.813 --> 00:05:14.230
In the past, you may have had
to configure your node pools

00:05:14.230 --> 00:05:16.150
to ensure that they
have enough capacity.

00:05:16.150 --> 00:05:18.550
But with Autopilot,
we do that for you.

00:05:18.550 --> 00:05:20.320
This takes about a
minute to provision,

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:21.878
so I'm just going to skip ahead.

00:05:21.878 --> 00:05:25.224
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:05:30.010 --> 00:05:32.650
Let's go ahead and grab the
IP address of this sample

00:05:32.650 --> 00:05:36.390
application and check it out.

00:05:36.390 --> 00:05:39.778
So I pasted that IP address
into my browser, loaded it up,

00:05:39.778 --> 00:05:41.070
and I can give it a quick test.

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:46.190
And that was Autopilot.

00:05:46.190 --> 00:05:47.700
With the introduction
of Autopilot,

00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:51.410
GKE is now available in two
modes of operation, standard

00:05:51.410 --> 00:05:52.700
and Autopilot.

00:05:52.700 --> 00:05:54.590
Standard is the
experience that we've

00:05:54.590 --> 00:05:56.180
been building since
the launch of GKE,

00:05:56.180 --> 00:05:57.830
where you have full
control over the nodes

00:05:57.830 --> 00:05:59.360
with the ability
to finetune things

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:00.590
to your heart's content--

00:06:00.590 --> 00:06:03.230
for example, running custom
administrative workloads--

00:06:03.230 --> 00:06:06.540
and even tweaking the node
operating system itself.

00:06:06.540 --> 00:06:09.087
Autopilot is a hands-off,
fully-managed solution

00:06:09.087 --> 00:06:10.920
that empowers you to
focus on your workloads

00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:12.420
while we take care of the rest.

00:06:12.420 --> 00:06:15.180
Both modes of operation are
generally available and fully

00:06:15.180 --> 00:06:17.790
supported by Google Cloud,
so please choose whichever

00:06:17.790 --> 00:06:19.530
best suits your needs.

00:06:19.530 --> 00:06:21.825
To learn more about Autopilot,
please follow this link

00:06:21.825 --> 00:06:24.060
to g.co/autopilot.

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:27.710
[MUSIC PLAYING]

